tangles, ordered from left to right.\n\nRule\nBuild a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\nversions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\nrectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\nconsecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\nbottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\nagain separated by one background column.\n\nKey insight\nThe input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\ncontributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\npreserving left-to-right object order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"solid monochromatic foreground rectangles ordered left-to-right\",\n  \"dst\": \"packed upper wide slots and lower tall slots in the same left-to-right order\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"rank(s) == rank(d) for both the upper and lower slot sequences\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each rectangle twice: min(h,w) by max(h,w) top-aligned in the upper band, and max(h,w) by min(h,w) bottom-aligned in the lower band, with one background column between slots\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height 10; width is the sum of max(h,w) over source rectangles plus one separator column between consecutive rectangles\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3905\nmechanism=0.4983\nlexical=0.0182\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:axis, object:rectangle, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e3fe1151.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e3fe1151.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"four equal corner regions divided by a solid background cross, each with one background-colored blank pixel\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill each blank with the color needed so every corner region has the same color multiset, using the maximum observed count of each color across the regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4029\nmechanism=0.5273\nlexical=0.0216\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/58490d8a.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/58490d8a.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"colored sprites s outside the black legend bbox\",\n  \"dst\": \"follow pixels d inside the black legend bbox (one per key color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"s reduces to 1 dot placed left-to-right starting at pos(d) with 1 bg pixel gap, replacing d\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db615bd4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3713\nmechanism=0.4706\nlexical=0.0166\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:axis, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/db615bd4.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/db615bd4.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nThicken the sparse frame and stamp the external sparse sprites into it in positional order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a regular lattice color, one large\nsparse rectangular frame, and one or more external monochrome sprites whose\npixels also sit on a two-step lattice.\n\nRule\nUse the largest sparse rectangular perimeter as the frame. Clear that frame\nregion and the external sprite pixels to background, draw a solid one-pixel\nborder in the frame color, solidify each sparse sprite into its contiguous\ntwo-step mask, and place the sprites inside the frame with equal gaps.\nWide frames receive sprites left-to-right by source position; tall frames\nreceive them top-to-bottom by source position.\n\nKey insight\nThe frame supplies ordered slots: the sprite-to-slot assignment is by visible\nsource order, not by numeric color or by matching individual cells.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"external sparse monochrome sprites s on the two-step lattice, ordered left-to-right for a wide frame or top-to-bottom for a tall frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"equal-gap interior slots d inside the large sparse rectangular frame, ordered along the frame long axis\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"rank(s) == rank(d)\",\n  \"transform\": \"clear the original sprite pixels; replace the sparse frame by a solid one-pixel border; expand each sprite's two-step mask to a contiguous mask before stamping\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"25c199f5\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3732\nmechanism=0.5083\nlexical=0.0179\ncode_feature=0.2000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/25c199f5.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/25c199f5.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPack the non-background rows from three separated panels into one panel, scanning panels right-to-left.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid contains three equal-size panels separated by two full-height divider columns.\n- The background is the dominant color; divider pixels use a color that appears only in the divider columns.\n\nRule:\n- Read panels from right to left, and within each panel read rows from top to bottom.\n- Keep each row that contains any non-background pixel, discarding all-background rows.\n- Place the kept rows at the bottom of a single panel-shaped output, padding above with background rows.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is row packing: the divider columns mark independent panels, and only the occupied rows are moved.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"non-background rows in three divider-separated panels\",\n  \"dst\": \"stack rows into one panel, ordered by panels right-to-left and rows top-to-bottom\",\n  \"transform\": \"discard all-background rows and top-pad with background to panel height\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"output is one panel\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5a719d11\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3343\nmechanism=0.4569\nlexical=0.0179\ncode_feature=0.1667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:axis, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, relation:aligned, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5a719d11.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/5a719d11.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\n\nRule:\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
